@Tutorial(time: 20) {
    @Intro(title: "Run Safe Codec Workflows") {
        Inspect, validate, decode, compare, and transcode DICOM pixel data through one in-memory API.

        ``DicomCodecWorkflowEngine`` returns versioned, PHI-free structured reports for every operation. Generated bytes
        stay paired with the report that describes how they were produced and validated.
    }

    @Section(title: "Discover live capabilities") {
        ``DicomCodecWorkflowEngine`` is a stateless, `Sendable` value. Its capability report describes the backends that
        are usable in the current process without exposing runtime-library paths.

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create the engine and select only backends that report themselves as available.

                @Code(name: "CodecCapabilities.swift", file: CodecCapabilities.swift)

                Availability is a live preflight result, not a promise that every object is decodable. Pixel shape,
                bit depth, photometric interpretation, encapsulation, and codestream details can still make a specific
                operation unsupported.
            }

            @Step {
                Render reports with the shared canonical renderer when they cross an adapter or automation boundary.

                @Code(name: "CodecReportRendering.swift", file: CodecReportRendering.swift)

                ``DicomCodecStructuredReport`` currently uses schema version 1. Its object and frame records omit
                patient, study, path, timestamp, and runtime-library-path fields. Do not add identifying source metadata
                around the report and then treat the combined payload as PHI-free.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Inspect and validate before decoding") {
        Inspection parses the object and reports pixel attributes, encapsulation, and syntax-eligible backends without
        decoding frames. Validation additionally checks pixel attributes, the Basic and Extended Offset Tables, and the
        declared frame mapping.

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Inspect an in-memory Part 10 object or parseable dataset.

                @Code(name: "CodecInspect.swift", file: CodecInspect.swift)

                The backends in an inspection report are eligible for the declared transfer syntax. They are not a
                per-frame qualification result and none is selected by inspection.
            }

            @Step {
                Check `success` after validation and preserve its diagnostics for the caller.

                @Code(name: "CodecValidate.swift", file: CodecValidate.swift)

                A parseable but inconsistent object returns a report whose `success` is `false`. Empty, unparseable,
                or otherwise invalid input throws ``DicomCodecWorkflowError`` instead. A nonthrowing call therefore does
                not by itself mean validation passed.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Decode selected frames") {
        Decode returns normalized raw pixel bytes and the report that records frame shape, hashes, selected backends,
        rollout decisions, and encapsulation details.

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Decode a zero-based frame selection. Pass `nil` to `frameIndexes` to decode every addressable frame.

                @Code(name: "CodecDecode.swift", file: CodecDecode.swift)

                The bytes for requested frames are concatenated in the order of `frameIndexes`. Use each entry in
                `result.report.frames` to interpret the corresponding frame shape and byte count. The artifact is not a
                DICOM file and has media type `application/vnd.dicom.decoded-pixels`.
            }

            @Step {
                Handle adapter-independent workflow errors by category instead of matching localized messages.

                @Code(name: "CodecErrorHandling.swift", file: CodecErrorHandling.swift)

                `consume`, `rejectInput`, and `reportUnavailableCapability` represent application-specific policy; the
                codec engine itself performs no file I/O or UI presentation.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Compare candidate and oracle pixels") {
        Candidate/oracle comparison is a qualification workflow for one JPEG-LS, JPEG 2000, or HTJ2K frame. It is not a
        general image comparison API and does not select a production fallback for the caller.

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Compare one zero-based frame and check the returned report.

                @Code(name: "CodecCompare.swift", file: CodecCompare.swift)

                A mismatch returns `success == false`, an `exact-decoded-pixels` comparison artifact, and the
                `codec.shadow.mismatch` diagnostic. An unsupported transfer syntax, unavailable candidate or oracle,
                invalid frame index, or corrupt frame throws a typed workflow error.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Transcode and verify a Part 10 artifact") {
        Transcoding accepts a complete Part 10 object and returns a complete Part 10 artifact. The engine verifies the
        destination transfer syntax, encapsulation and frame mapping, pixel attributes, and decoded output by default.

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Use reversible intent and keep decoded-pixel verification enabled for a lossless rewrite.

                @Code(name: "CodecTranscode.swift", file: CodecTranscode.swift)

                Reversible output must decode to the exact source bytes. Irreversible and JPEG-LS near-lossless
                intents currently verify decoded frame shape, not perceptual fidelity. Apply a separately qualified
                quality policy before using lossy output.
            }

            @Step {
                Inspect the artifact report before persisting or forwarding the generated bytes.

                @Code(name: "CodecPersistArtifact.swift", file: CodecPersistArtifact.swift)

                Do not disable `verifyDecodedPixels` merely because an output decoder is unavailable. Disabling it
                removes decoded source/output comparison and is appropriate only for an explicitly qualified external
                workflow.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Respect codec qualification boundaries") {
        The workflow API and schema-versioned reports are public production surfaces. Individual codec routes still have
        their own qualification status and runtime requirements.

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Keep rollout configuration outside application examples. `forced-for-tests` exists only for tests;
                candidate and oracle selection belongs to DicomCore.

                JPEG XL remains experimental and disabled by default. It requires explicit experimental policy and
                is not qualified as a sole clinical archive representation. JPEG 2000 output above 8 bits must not fall
                back to ImageIO, and HTJ2K never uses the ImageIO JPEG 2000 path. Missing precision-preserving or
                verification backends must remain typed failures rather than trigger an implicit lower-fidelity retry.
            }

            @Step {
                Query capabilities for observability, call the requested operation, and handle its structured report
                or typed error. This preserves the engine's validation, fallback identity, and audit trail without
                duplicating private routing policy in an app or command-line adapter.
            }
        }
    }
}
